\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Continuous functions of compact reduced links}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For the circle orbit of #{U(1)} and the unit-sphere orbit of
#{SU(2)} in \ref{fdip-0116}, give the joint orbit space its
quotient topology. The group-valued \vocabk{framed path
transport}{fdip-011B} on each chosen oriented edge gives a homeomorphism
from that space to the compact space #{G^E}. Restriction to the
constant reference scalar identifies the algebra of continuous
gauge-invariant real observables with the algebra of all
continuous real functions on #{G^E}.}

\proof{
\p{The coordinate homeomorphism of \ref{fdip-0116} identifies
joint configurations with site frames and reduced links. A gauge
acts by left multiplication on each site frame and fixes every
reduced link. The projection to the reduced tuple is continuous
and constant on each orbit, so it induces a continuous map from
the quotient by the definition of its topology. The inverse sends
a link tuple to the orbit of that tuple with constant reference
scalar. It is continuous as the composite of the constant-frame
inclusion and the quotient projection. Each tuple of site frames
can be gauged to the identity, giving both inverse identities.
Finite products of the compact group are compact.}

\p{A continuous invariant function descends continuously by
the same quotient property. Conversely a continuous function on
#{G^E} pulls back along the reduced-link projection. These maps
preserve sums, products and constants.}
}

\p{Continuity of the descended invariant function is formalized by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.continuous_orbitRelQuotientLift_iff}.}
